calls.scm        sorcerer's own (not vendored): `T{…}` composite literals as `@reference.call`.
lists.scm        sorcerer's own (not vendored): `@parameters` and `@arguments`, the lists anchors put items into.
placeholders.scm sorcerer's own (not vendored): `@placeholder`, `panic("hole …")` and `panic("not implemented")`.
preamble.scm     sorcerer's own (not vendored): `@preamble`, the `package` clause.
imports.scm      sorcerer's own (not vendored): `@import.path`, an import's path (for the "end of imports" anchor; conjure has no `[imports]` table for Go, see below).
# changes (sorcerer evaluates #eq?, #not-eq?, #match?, #not-match?, #any-of? and #not-any-of? and
# nothing else; a pattern using another predicate or directive is removed):
#   tags.scm: the two doc-comment patterns (`(comment)* @doc . (function_declaration …)`, and the
#   method one) use #strip! and #set-adjacent!; each is replaced by its definition pattern alone,
#   without the `@doc` comments. The patterns that capture only a `@name` and no definition or call
#   (`@reference.type`, the package name, imports, var, const, interface and struct type names) are
#   dropped: nothing reads them.
#   textobjects.scm: `(block (_) @statement.outer)` became `(statement_list (_) @statement.outer)`:
#   this grammar's block holds one statement_list, so the upstream pattern captured the list, and
#   an empty-body check (`stub`) needs the statements themselves.
# Go `[imports]` is left out: an import names a module path (a folder of files, under a prefix
# only go.mod knows), not a file.
